Package: Pando
Version: 2.0.11
Type: Package
Title: Inferring regulomes from multi-modal single-cell genomics data
Authors@R: person(given = "Jonas Simon",
               family = "Fleck",
               role = c("aut", "cre"),
               email = "jonas.fleck@bsse.ethz.ch",
               comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-4686-7254"))
Maintainer: Jonas Simon Fleck <jonas.fleck@bsse.ethz.ch>
Description: Pando jointly uses scRNA-seq and scATAC-seq measurements to infer
    regulatory relationships between TF-binding site pairs and target genes.
    For two or more conditions within a broad cell type, infer_condition_grn
    retains the original Pando regulatory-domain, motif, peak-target-correlation
    and TF-target-correlation candidate logic while producing directly
    comparable condition coefficients. Candidate discovery is run on all
    eligible-condition cells pooled together and independently within each
    condition. Exact target-TF-region triples passing both configured correlation
    gates in the pooled/global calculation or in at least one condition are
    deduplicated into one frozen common dictionary. Every condition uses this
    identical ordered dictionary and the original TF-by-ATAC interaction
    predictor with an equal-condition within-condition RMS predictor scale.
    Production condition coefficients are estimated jointly by E-star with the
    fixed deviation threshold z=0.25. The Q-orthogonal shared component is the
    unpenalized joint MLE; only identifiable condition-deviation coordinates
    receive the information-scaled sparse penalty. Zero-information contrasts
    are fixed to sharing as a boundary convention and are distinguished from
    identifiable deviations set exactly to zero by the penalty. E-star fusion
    metadata are not reused for statistical selection. Formal inference is run
    separately, without fusion, by fitting the complete frozen target dictionary
    independently within each condition using a Gaussian linear model with an
    intercept. Coefficient tests use finite residual-degree-of-freedom t tests;
    aliased condition coefficients are marked non-estimable rather than treated
    as zero. Each exact edge then receives one omnibus test across its estimable
    conditions: a one-condition edge inherits the corresponding exact t-test P
    value, whereas edges estimable in multiple independent conditions use a
    multivariate Wald chi-square statistic. Benjamini-Hochberg correction is
    applied once across all estimable exact edges in the broad-cell-type network.
    The resulting topology is common to every retained condition, while every
    admitted condition keeps its own continuous E-star production coefficient.
    Full-data target R-squared remains diagnostic and is not a conditional-GRN
    topology gate. Standard infer_grn retains its independent Gaussian GLM and
    single-task ridge implementations. Target-parallel candidate discovery and
    fitting use target-specific payload batches, and independent cell-type jobs
    can be dispatched through BiocParallel.
